## Why This Is Asked

Understanding data leakage and proper evaluation on small datasets is critical for reliable models. This question tests practical thinking, not theory alone, and guards against tempting but flawed splits that could invalidate results.

## Key Concepts

- Data leakage and temporal splits
- Baseline models and feature encoding
- Evaluation metrics and calibration
- Time-aware validation strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple logistic regression gradient step (conceptual)
function step(w, b, x, y, lr) {
  const z = 1 / (1 + Math.exp(-(dot(w, x) + b)));
  const error = y - z;
  // Update weights and bias
  for (let i = 0; i < w.length; i++) {
    w[i] += lr * error * x[i];
  }
  b += lr * error;
  return { w, b };
}
```

This example demonstrates the core gradient descent logic while keeping the implementation minimal and focused on the mathematical foundation.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical lightweight baseline building for text routing under strict latency constraints, in a data-scarce regime with model calibration requirements. Also covers drift monitoring and retraining cadence.

## Key Concepts
- Lightweight text representation with a fixed 30-feature vector
- SGD-based multiclass logistic regression or tiny decision tree for efficiency
- Calibration via temperature scaling
- Drift detection using rolling holdout validation
- Deployment latency and monitoring

## Code Example
```javascript
// Training loop outline for SGD multiclass LR
const model = new SGDLogisticRegression({
  learningRate: 0.01,
  epochs: 100,
  batchSize: 32
});
model.fit(XTrain, yTrain);
// Temperature scaling for calibration
const temp = calibrateTemperature(model, XVal, yVal);
// Inference
const predict = (features) => {
  const start = performance.now();
  const probs = model.predictProb(features, temp);
  const latency = performance.now() - start;
  return { probs, latency };
};
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests on-device constraints, lightweight models, quantization, calibration, drift detection, and retraining cadence. Demonstrates integration of hardware constraints with ML basics.

## Key Concepts
- Edge ML constraints, memory, power
- Model compression (8-bit quantization, pruning)
- Feature hashing vs dense input
- Calibration and drift monitoring
- Data splits for on-device eval

## Code Example
```javascript
// 8-bit quantization sketch
function quantizeWeights(weights){
  const maxAbs = Math.max(...weights.map(Math.abs), 1);
  const scale = 127 / maxAbs;
  return weights.map(w => Math.round(w * scale));
}
```

